The Purpose of DHCP :
	
		* DHCP is used to allow hosts to automatically/dynamically learn various aspects of their network 		  configuration such as :
			- Ip address
			- Subnet mask
			- Default Gateway
			- DNS server

		* It is an essential part of modern networks

		* Typically used for client 'client devices' such as workstations (PCs), Phones

		* Devices such as routers, servers , etc are usually manualy configured

		* In small networks (such as home networks) the router typically acts as the DHCP server for hosts 	          in the LAN

		* In larger networks, the DHCP server is usually a windows / linux server

Basic Functions Of DHCP :

		* DHCP server 'lease' IP address to clients
			- These leases are usually not permanent, and the client must up the address at the end of 			  the lease

		 * DHCP Release 
			- client tells the server its done using its current assigned IP address
				- DHCP server uses UDP port 67
				- DHCP client uses UDP port 68	

		  * DHCP Renew 
			- Client ask server to renew its Ip configuration using DHCP	

		  * DHCP Discover
			- Client send out a ICMP broadcast message in order to discover a DHCP server and ask for
			  an ip address from that server

		  * DHCP Offer
			- A DHCP server receive the DHCP discover request coming from the client and offer an ip 			  address as a result 	

		  * DHCP Request
			- The PC send a unicast message to the server asking to use the Ip address the DHCP server
			  offered 

		  * DHCP Ack
			- The DHCP server send an acknowledgement message to the client telling it, its permitted 			  to use the requested IP address

		  * DHCP D-O-R-A

				Discover		Client -> Server 		Broadcast
				
				Offer			Server -> Client		Broadcast or Unicast

				Request			Client -> Server		Broadcast

				Ack			Server -> Client		Broadcast or Unicast
		
				Release			Client -> Server		Unicast

		   * DHCP Relay
			- Some network engineers might choose to configure each router to act as the DHCP server 			  for its connected LANs

			- However, Large enterprises often choose to use a centralized DHCP server

			- If the server is centralized, it wont receive the DHCP clients broadcast DHCP messages
				# Broadcast messages dont leave the local subnet

				- To fix this, you can configure a router to act as a DHCP Relay Agent

				- The Router will forward the clients broadcast DHCP messages to the remote DHCP 				  server as unicast messages
			
					ex :
						   Src:XXXX  Dst:YYYY          Src:0000 Dst:ffff
						<-------------------------    <------------------  Discover
							Src:YYYY Dst:XXXX	   Src:XXXX Dst:ffff
						Offer ---------------------->   --------------------->
						   Src:XXXX  Dst:YYYY		Src:0000 Dst:ffff
						<-----------------------    <---------------------  Request
						      Src:YYYY Dst:XXXX		   Src:XXXX Dst:ffff
						Ack ------------------------->    ---------------------->

Configuration Of DHCP server :

		- R1(config)#ip dhcp excluded-address 192.168.1.1 192.168.1.10 --> Range that wont be used by DHCP clients
		
		- R1(config)#ip dhcp pool <NAME> ---> create DHCP POOL

		- R1(dhcp-config)#network <NETWORK /SUBNET> ---> specify the subnet of addresses to be assigned to client

		- R1(dhcp-config)#dns-server 8.8.8.8 ---> specify the DNS server that DHCP clients should use

		- R1(dhcp-config)#domain-name <name of domain> ---> specify the domain name of the network

		- R1(dhcp-config)#default-router 192.168.1.1 ---> Specify the default gateway

		- R1(dhcp-config)#lease 0 5 30 Specify the lease time or lease infine
					| | |---> minutes	
				       Day|
					  |
					Hours

		* DHCP Replay Agent configuration

			- R1(config)# int g0/1 ---> configure the int connected to the subnet of the client devices
			
			- R1(config-if)#ip helper-address 192.168.10.10 ---> configure the IP address of the DHCP server as the 			  helper address 

